    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Add Art 4-E §§71-aa - 71-dd, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7348\n\nSPONSOR: ALCANTARA\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to enacting\nthe \"surplus food to charitable organizations act\"\n\n \nPURPOSE OF THE BILL:\n\nIn response to the humanitarian and environmental concerns outlined\nbelow, the purpose of this bill is to provide a vehicle for large super-\nmarkets to make available edible food they are throwing out, to charita-\nble organizations which feed the poor, needy, disadvantaged and at-risk\npersons. This is edible food the supermarket is throwing away in the\nnormal course of its business operations. The supermarkets are not\nrequired to transport or distribute the food.  State tax revenues and\npublic health laws and regulations are unaffected.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: Enacts the Surplus Food to Charitable Organizations Act.\n\nSection 2: Adds a new Article 4-E to the Agriculture and Markets Act to\nestablish the Surplus Food to Charitable Organizations Act.\n\n- Creates a new Agriculture and Markets section 71-aa to define the\nterms \"excess food\", \"qualifying charity\" and \"supermarket\".\n\nUnder the provisions of this bill, the term \"excess food\" shall mean\nedible foods offered for sale by supermarkets which has become surplus\nand which such supermarket plans to throw out. Such foods shall not\ninclude fresh milk, meat, fish, or poultry, damaged food, or food\nsubject to a governmental or producer recall.\n\n\"Qualifying Charity\" shall mean a bona fide religious, charitable, or\nnon-for-profit organization that provides food at no cost to the poor,\nneedy, disadvantaged or at-risk persons. Such programs can include food\npantries, food banks, soup kitchens, or community based programs that\nprovide food to such persons.\n\n\"Supermarket\" means a retail store that has more than 10,000 square feet\nof space at a given location devoted to the sale of foods, food stuffs,\nor groceries. Supermarkets shall not include cafeterias, bakeries,\ncaterers, hospitals, group homes, drug stores, or food courts.\n\n- Adds a new section 71-bb to add a Declaration of Policy section. To\nhelp address the large need to supply foods for the poor, needy, and\ndisadvantaged, it is the stated policy of the State of New York to\nencourage, wherever possible, supermarkets to make available excess\nedible food to qualifying charitable organizations.\n\n- Adds a new Section 71-cc to state that every supermarket, to the best\nof their ability, is to make a reasonable effort to make available on\nits premises its excess foods (before it is thrown out) to charitable\norganizations who have requested in writing to pick up excess food.  The\nbill places no obligation on supermarkets to transport or distribute\nsuch excess foods. Supermarkets may impose restrictions to ensure pick-\n\nup does not interfere with its business operations.  Section 71-dd also\nstates all public health laws and regulations remain in place.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn the United States, 60 to 100 million tons of edible food is thrown\nout each year. However, this food is critically needed as an estimated\n50 Million of our residents, including 16 Million children do not have\nsufficient food to eat. This bill provides a mechanism in statute to\nclearly authorize and reduce any questions or concerns that supermarkets\nmay have regarding a way to make available food to charitable organiza-\ntions that sponsor food pantries, food banks, soup kitchens or other\ncommunity based programs that feed our poor and disadvantaged residents.\n\nAdditionally, this bill will help to reduce the amount of food stuffs\nthat are transferred to our municipal solid waste facilities. It is very\nexpensive to operate solid waste management facilities. This bill should\nhelp to reduce the volume of trash that is transported to and deposited\nin such solid waste management facilities. This will not only benefit\nthe environment by reducing harmful emissions, but help to reduce munic-\nipal costs in operating such facilities and the burden that this places\non municipal taxpayers.\n\nThis bill will help the environment in other ways. The cultivation,\nharvesting, and transport of food, food stuffs, and groceries is a very\nenergy and water intensive endeavor. Approximately 25% of water used in\nthe United States goes to produce foods which end up in landfills. All\nfood that is grown, harvested, and transported to market that is not\nconsumed is not only food that is wasted in the nutrition that it could\nhave provided to our disadvantaged, but it is energy and water wasted.\nBy minimizing the amount of food that is throw away, that is a net\nsavings in the amount of energy and water that is expended to produce\nsuch food.\n\nThis bill should have two direct effects on the reduction of greenhouse\ngas emissions from New York State. Firstly, the bill will reduce the\namount of food waste that is being transported to sites that are part of\nthe state's solid waste disposal system, governed by the Department of\nEnvironmental Conservation. These include the 174 permitted and 394\nregistered transfer stations throughout the state, and the 27 Municipal\nSolid Waste Landfills, located in DEC Regions 4-9. This transport is\ncarried out by trucks, which emit greenhouse gases, in the form of large\nquantities of carbon dioxide, as well as smaller amounts of methane and\nnitrous oxide. Secondly, the bill will reduce the volume of organic\nwaste in the disposal sites. As this waste decays, it releases roughly\nequal amounts of carbon dioxide and methane. The latter is particularly\nserious, since methane is over 25 times more detrimental than carbon\ndioxide.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew Bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis law shall take effect 180 days after it shall have become law.\n",
